<!-- INTRO BOX                                                     -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="intro-box">
  <h3>&#128218; What is this?</h3>
  <p>AI Hedge Fund is an open-source Python project that simulates a hedge fund run
  entirely by AI. Instead of a single monolithic model, it uses <strong>19 specialized
  analyst agents</strong> &mdash; each embodying the investment philosophy of a famous investor
  (Warren Buffett, Charlie Munger, Ben Graham, Nassim Taleb, and more). These agents
  independently analyze stocks using real financial data, then pass their signals to a
  <strong>risk manager</strong> and <strong>portfolio manager</strong> who make the final
  buy/sell/short decisions.</p>
  <p>The system is orchestrated by <strong>LangGraph</strong> (a LangChain framework for
  stateful, graph-based agent workflows) and supports 12+ LLM providers including
  OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, Groq, and local models. It also includes a
  backtesting engine to evaluate strategies against historical data.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>&#128161; Analogy: Think of it like&hellip;</summary>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem;">Imagine you have a <strong>board of investment advisors</strong>
    sitting around a conference table. Each advisor is a world-class investor with their own
    philosophy: Warren Buffett looks for undervalued companies with strong moats, Nassim Taleb
    watches for tail risks and black swan events, while the Technical Analyst reads chart
    patterns and momentum indicators.</p>
    <p>You give them all the same stock ticker (say, AAPL). Each advisor independently studies
    the company using real financial data &mdash; balance sheets, income statements, insider
    trades, analyst estimates &mdash; and writes a memo with their signal: <em>bullish</em>,
    <em>bearish</em>, or <em>neutral</em>, along with a confidence score and reasoning.</p>
    <p>All these memos land on the desk of a <strong>risk manager</strong>, who checks whether
    the combined position would blow up the portfolio (too much concentration, too much risk).
    Then a <strong>portfolio manager</strong> reads everything, weighs the opinions, and decides
    exactly how many shares to buy, sell, or short.</p>
    <p>That&rsquo;s AI Hedge Fund &mdash; except every advisor is an LLM prompted with the
    philosophy and heuristics of the real investor they represent.</p>
  </details>
</div>

