<!-- ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM                                          -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="architecture">Architecture</h2>
<p>The LangGraph <code>StateGraph</code> orchestrates the full analysis pipeline. Analyst
agents run in parallel, then results converge through risk management and portfolio
allocation before producing final trading decisions.</p>
<div class="diagram-container">
<pre class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  START(["&#9654; start_node"]) --> PA["Parallel<br/>Analysts"]

  subgraph Famous["Famous Investors"]
    WB["Warren Buffett<br/>Agent"]
    CM["Charlie Munger<br/>Agent"]
    BG["Ben Graham<br/>Agent"]
    NT["Nassim Taleb<br/>Agent"]
    SC["Stanley Druckenmiller<br/>Agent"]
    PL["Peter Lynch<br/>Agent"]
    BF["Bill Ackman<br/>Agent"]
    JM["Joel Greenblatt<br/>Agent"]
    CA["Cathie Wood<br/>Agent"]
    MG["Michael Burry<br/>Agent"]
  end

  subgraph Analytical["Analytical Agents"]
    TA["Technical<br/>Analyst"]
    FA["Fundamentals<br/>Analyst"]
    SA["Sentiment<br/>Analyst"]
    VA["Valuation<br/>Analyst"]
  end

  subgraph Decision["Decision Pipeline"]
    RM["Risk<br/>Manager"]
    PM["Portfolio<br/>Manager"]
  end

  PA --> WB & CM & BG & NT & SC & PL & BF & JM & CA & MG
  PA --> TA & FA & SA & VA
  WB & CM & BG & NT & SC & PL & BF & JM & CA & MG --> RM
  TA & FA & SA & VA --> RM
  RM --> PM
  PM --> END_NODE(["&#9632; END"])
</pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- DATA FLOW DIAGRAM                                             -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="data-flow">Data Flow</h2>
<p>A complete trading cycle from CLI invocation through data fetching, parallel
analysis, risk checks, and final order generation.</p>
<div class="diagram-container">
<pre class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
  participant CLI as CLI / main.py
  participant SG as StateGraph
  participant API as Financial APIs
  participant Agents as Analyst Agents (parallel)
  participant RM as Risk Manager
  participant PM as Portfolio Manager

  CLI->>SG: run_hedge_fund(tickers, dates, model)
  SG->>API: Fetch price data & fundamentals
  API-->>SG: OHLCV, financials, insider trades
  SG->>Agents: Dispatch state to all analysts
  Note over Agents: 19 agents run in parallel
  Agents->>API: Fetch additional metrics per agent
  API-->>Agents: Balance sheets, ratios, news
  Agents-->>SG: analyst_signals (signal, confidence, reasoning)
  SG->>RM: Aggregate signals + portfolio state
  RM-->>SG: risk_assessment (approved positions, limits)
  SG->>PM: risk_assessment + analyst_signals
  PM-->>SG: trading_decisions (action, quantity, rationale)
  SG-->>CLI: Final portfolio decisions
  Note over CLI: Display decisions or feed to backtester
</pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- NARRATED WALKTHROUGH                                          -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="walkthrough-box">
<h3>&#128694; Step-by-Step Narrated Walkthrough</h3>
<p style="color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;">Follow a single <strong>stock
analysis request for AAPL</strong> as it flows through the entire system:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>CLI Invocation</strong> &mdash; The user runs <code>python src/main.py
  --ticker AAPL</code> (or uses the Streamlit UI). The CLI parses arguments: ticker
  list, date range, initial cash, model provider, and analyst selection.</li>
  <li><strong>Graph Construction</strong> &mdash; <code>run_hedge_fund()</code> in
  <code>graph/state.py</code> builds a LangGraph <code>StateGraph</code>. Each selected
  analyst is registered as a parallel node. Risk manager and portfolio manager are
  sequential nodes downstream.</li>
  <li><strong>State Initialization</strong> &mdash; An <code>AgentState</code> TypedDict
  is created with tickers, date range, portfolio (cash, positions), and empty signal
  containers. This state object flows through every node.</li>
  <li><strong>Price Data Fetch</strong> &mdash; The <code>tools/api.py</code> module
  calls financial APIs (Yahoo Finance, Financial Modeling Prep) to retrieve OHLCV price
  history, balance sheets, income statements, and insider trading data for AAPL.</li>
  <li><strong>Parallel Analyst Dispatch</strong> &mdash; LangGraph fans out the state
  to all 19 analyst agents simultaneously. Each agent receives the same state snapshot
  but applies its own investment philosophy.</li>
  <li><strong>Agent Analysis</strong> &mdash; Warren Buffett&rsquo;s agent examines
  owner earnings, moat indicators, and management quality. Ben Graham&rsquo;s agent
  checks the margin of safety and net-net value. The Technical Analyst runs momentum,
  RSI, and MACD calculations. Each agent calls an LLM with a tailored system prompt.</li>
  <li><strong>Signal Generation</strong> &mdash; Each agent returns a structured signal:
  <code>{signal: "bullish"|"bearish"|"neutral", confidence: 0.0-1.0, reasoning: "..."}</code>.
  These are collected into <code>state["analyst_signals"]</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Signal Aggregation</strong> &mdash; All 19 signals reconverge at the risk
  manager node. The framework waits for every parallel branch to complete before
  proceeding.</li>
  <li><strong>Risk Assessment</strong> &mdash; The risk manager agent evaluates the
  aggregate signal against portfolio constraints: maximum position size, sector
  concentration, correlation limits, and overall portfolio risk. It outputs approved
  positions with size limits.</li>
  <li><strong>Portfolio Decision</strong> &mdash; The portfolio manager receives both
  the raw analyst signals and the risk assessment. It decides the final action for AAPL
  (buy 150 shares, sell all, short 50 shares, or hold) along with detailed reasoning.</li>
  <li><strong>Decision Output</strong> &mdash; The final <code>trading_decisions</code>
  dict is written to state. If running live, decisions are printed to the console in a
  formatted table. If backtesting, they feed into the backtester engine.</li>
  <li><strong>Backtesting (optional)</strong> &mdash; The <code>backtester.py</code>
  engine replays the strategy over historical date ranges, executing decisions day by
  day and tracking portfolio value, drawdown, Sharpe ratio, and benchmark comparison.</li>
</ol>
</div>

